That Marshall hears.
马歇尔听到了。

00:45:43.989 --> 00:45:44.989
Yeah, that's right.
是的，没错。

00:45:44.989 --> 00:45:46.543
So just a shout out to Janet.
所以只是向珍妮特大声喊叫。

00:45:46.543 --> 00:45:47.239
That's right.
这是正确的。

00:45:47.239 --> 00:45:47.989
We love Janet.
我们爱珍妮特。

00:45:47.989 --> 00:45:58.989
I will say that I am not a fan of the gesture that Ted is a fan of over the top.
我要说的是，我不喜欢特德所喜欢的过度行为。

00:45:58.989 --> 00:46:02.989
Like hiring a blue string quartet.
就像雇用一个蓝色弦乐四重奏一样。

00:46:02.989 --> 00:46:11.989
Plus, how many roses there had to have been, like, eight dozen roses, and, like, he's an architect.
另外，那里必须有多少玫瑰，比如，八打玫瑰，而且，比如，他是一名建筑师。

00:46:11.989 --> 00:46:12.989
He's a young architect.
他是一位年轻的建筑师。

00:46:12.989 --> 00:46:15.110
He's not making that much money.
他赚不到那么多钱。

00:46:15.110 --> 00:46:18.710
Like, it's an absurd gesture.
就像，这是一个荒谬的举动。

00:46:18.710 --> 00:46:23.590
And I think that her kind of allergy to it is appropriate.
我认为她对此的过敏是适当的。

00:46:23.590 --> 00:46:27.550
And it's one of those things when people ask me, like, what do I love about him?
当人们问我时，这就是其中之一，比如，我爱他什么？

00:46:27.550 --> 00:46:28.750
What do I hate about him?
我讨厌他什么？

00:46:28.750 --> 00:46:31.190
This is something I don't particularly like about him.
这是我特别不喜欢他的一点。

00:46:31.190 --> 00:46:32.949
I think it's over the top.
我认为这太过分了。

00:46:32.949 --> 00:46:39.409
I think it's, I think it's a little creepy and I think it's, it's sometimes really misplaced.
我认为，我认为这有点令人毛骨悚然，我认为，有时确实放错了地方。

00:46:39.409 --> 00:46:48.650
Like he, the, the, the, the 10 I like is, um, my gesture is, I put on a bleach stained sweatshirt and I come to your cupcake shop and I kiss you.
就像他一样，我喜欢的10是，嗯，我的手势是，我穿上一件漂白的运动衫，我来到你的纸杯蛋糕店，我吻你。

00:46:49.130 --> 00:46:53.389
Like that to me is just, is just more.
对我来说这只是，只是更多。

00:46:53.389 --> 00:47:00.372
I think he, um, because he started the robin adventure with this grand gesture of stealing the blue French horn.
我认为他，嗯，因为他以偷蓝色圆号的宏伟姿态开始了知更鸟的冒险。

00:47:00.372 --> 00:47:06.233
I think he tries to up the ante and it's just something that makes my skin crawl a little bit.
我认为他试图提高赌注，这让我有点起鸡皮疙瘩。

00:47:06.233 --> 00:47:07.730
I just want to say that.
我只想这么说。

00:47:07.730 --> 00:47:08.230
I agree.
我同意。

00:47:08.230 --> 00:47:14.166
Well, I think you notice in the season one, like when he tries to do the perfect thing, it doesn't work.
好吧，我想你在第一季就注意到了，就像当他试图做完美的事情时，却行不通。

00:47:14.166 --> 00:47:17.704
When he puts on the sweatshirt with the bleach stain, it does.
当他穿上那件沾有漂白剂污渍的运动衫时，情况就这样了。

00:47:17.704 --> 00:47:18.204
Yeah.
是的。

00:47:18.204 --> 00:47:32.188
And even in this one, this gesture does not work, but the sort of messy and insane and unhinged thing that is not at all traditionally romantic, the rain dance, the act of faith and asking the universe for help, that's what works, not the roses.
即使在这一次，这种姿态也不起作用，但那种凌乱、疯狂、精神错乱的东西，一点也不传统的浪漫，雨舞，信仰的行为和向宇宙寻求帮助，这才是有效的，而不是玫瑰。

00:47:32.188 --> 00:47:34.757
I feel like there's interesting lesson there.
我觉得那里有一个有趣的教训。

00:47:34.757 --> 00:47:37.668
And I think it's in accord with what you just said.
我认为这与你刚才所说的相符。

00:47:37.409 --> 00:47:46.592
I also think there's, I remember you guys said that Ted was pitched as Lloyd Dobler meets George Bailey from It's A Wonderful Life.
我还认为，我记得你们说过，泰德是在《生活多美好》中饰演劳埃德·多布勒遇见乔治·贝利的。

00:47:46.592 --> 00:47:54.724
And I think that there's a thing you can watch on YouTube of like all the times that John Cusack has been rained on.
我认为你可以在 YouTube 上观看约翰·库萨克 (John Cusack) 一直受到关注的一件事。

00:47:54.724 --> 00:47:59.560
I think he's been one of the most rained on actors in cinema history.
我认为他是电影史上最受关注的演员之一。

00:47:59.560 --> 00:48:06.010
And I think that was a little wink to the Cusakian film bio that he was getting just doused.
我认为这对库萨基安的电影简历来说有点眨眼，他刚刚被浇灭了。

00:48:06.010 --> 00:48:07.010
Absolutely.
绝对地。

00:48:07.010 --> 00:48:16.530
I mean, there's just such a power to seeing you out there in the rain, that big crane shot, the big jib shot looking down on you, and you're getting fully doused, and you are really soaked.
我的意思是，在雨中看到你在外面，那种巨大的起重机镜头，巨大的三角臂镜头俯视着你，你全身都被淋湿了，你真的湿透了，这是一种力量。

00:48:16.530 --> 00:48:18.130
You're looking up, it's getting in your eyes.
你抬起头，它就进入了你的眼睛。

00:48:18.130 --> 00:48:26.849
You can't replicate the performance that you achieved in that moment partially because of that rain and going into your eyes and washing down your face.
你无法复制那一刻所取得的表现，部分原因是那场雨进入了你的眼睛并冲刷了你的脸。

00:48:26.849 --> 00:48:28.409
It really changes the energy, doesn't it?
它确实改变了能量，不是吗？

00:48:28.409 --> 00:48:42.670
I always loved, one, we've talked about it, going down to New York Street, where you would just, you know, you would either take a golf cart, or I like actually walking down to New York Street, but it was like a eight to 10 minutes stroll down to New York Street from stage 22.
我一直很喜欢，我们已经讨论过，去纽约街，在那里你要么坐高尔夫球车，要么我喜欢实际步行到纽约街，但这就像从 22 赛段步行 8 到 10 分钟到纽约街。

00:48:42.670 --> 00:48:44.710
And you're in Southern California.
而你在南加州。

00:48:44.710 --> 00:48:47.349
It's almost never raining like that.
几乎从来没有下过这样的雨。

00:48:47.349 --> 00:48:56.230
So they would bring in this huge tanker of rainwater and they just wouldn't make it rain on the stage.
所以他们会带来这么大的雨水罐，但他们不会让舞台下雨。

00:48:56.230 --> 00:49:03.750
And I always felt like when I was on the stage getting, and when I was on New York Street getting rained on, I felt like I've made it in show business.
我总是觉得，当我在舞台上，当我在纽约街头被雨淋湿时，我感觉我已经在演艺界取得了成功。

00:49:03.750 --> 00:49:10.750
Like there was such a feeling of like old, tiny Hollywood, you know, New York Street getting rained on.
就像有一种古老的小好莱坞的感觉，你知道，纽约街道被雨淋湿了。

00:49:10.750 --> 00:49:17.550
It was such a sweet, cool feeling of make believe, but real, like it was just, I just loved it.
这是一种甜蜜、酷炫的感觉，但又真实，就像它只是一样，我就是喜欢它。

00:49:17.550 --> 00:49:22.590
So I think part of my joy is in the character, I'm in the moment.
所以我认为我的部分快乐在于角色，我活在当下。

00:49:22.590 --> 00:49:25.909
It's the Hollywood of the end of PB's big adventure.
这是 PB 大冒险结束时的好莱坞。

00:49:25.909 --> 00:49:26.750
Yeah.
是的。

00:49:26.750 --> 00:49:33.550
I'm in the joy of the character, but I'm also in the joy of the actor getting to do this fun thing.
我很高兴扮演这个角色，但我也很高兴演员能够做这件有趣的事情。

00:49:33.550 --> 00:49:36.429
Just to point out, Alexis Dennisov is wonderful in this episode.
只是要指出，亚历克西斯·丹尼索夫在这一集中表现出色。

00:49:36.429 --> 00:49:45.309
So is Amy Acker, who plays Barney's quote unquote X, who he dated twice, who knows about Native American culture, and is studying it in grad school.
艾米·阿克（Amy Acker）也是如此，她扮演巴尼的引言X，他约会过两次，她了解美洲原住民文化，并且正在研究生院学习它。

00:49:45.309 --> 00:49:51.989
And she is from the show, Angel, where she was a co-star and eventually love interest of Alexis' character on Angel.
她来自电视剧《天使》，她是该剧的联合主演，并最终爱上了《天使》中亚历克西斯的角色。

00:49:51.989 --> 00:49:53.190
Angels to spin off from Buffy.
天使从巴菲身上剥离出来。

00:49:53.190 --> 00:49:54.710
I love both shows, started Carter.
卡特开始说，我喜欢这两部剧。

00:49:54.710 --> 00:49:58.949
So it was wonderful to have an angel mini reunion in this episode.
所以在这一集中天使迷你重聚真是太棒了。

00:49:58.949 --> 00:50:03.349
And also for the sharp-eyed vampire Lou is of course Lou the weatherman.
对于目光敏锐的吸血鬼卢来说，当然还有天气预报员卢。

00:50:03.349 --> 00:50:05.550
She really, she was wonderful, Amy.
她真的，她很棒，艾米。

00:50:05.550 --> 00:50:07.150
Jordana even said, I really like her.
乔丹娜甚至说，我真的很喜欢她。

00:50:07.150 --> 00:50:09.030
She's great, you know?
她很棒，你知道吗？

00:50:09.030 --> 00:50:11.469
I just thought she was such a winning presence.
我只是觉得她是一个如此迷人的存在。

00:50:11.469 --> 00:50:17.949
And really, she gave it to Barney in a really funny way that I thought was really quite charming.
事实上，她以一种非常有趣的方式把它交给了巴尼，我认为这真的很迷人。

00:50:17.949 --> 00:50:24.321
I loved when Barney said, you're forcing me to be the voice of reason it's not a good look for me.
我喜欢巴尼说的，你强迫我发出理性的声音，这对我来说不太好看。

00:50:24.321 --> 00:50:25.232
I loved Craig.
我爱克雷格。

00:50:25.232 --> 00:50:26.142
I loved pause.
我喜欢暂停。

00:50:26.142 --> 00:50:27.832
Where did pause come from?
停顿从何而来？

00:50:27.832 --> 00:50:33.749
It's such a, it's actually quite a smart relationship tactic, but where did that come from?
这确实是一个非常聪明的关系策略，但是它是从哪里来的呢？

00:50:33.749 --> 00:50:34.790
Do you remember?
你是否记得？

00:50:34.789 --> 00:50:37.683
It is a very, it is a very good relationship tactic.
这是一个非常非常好的关系策略。

00:50:37.683 --> 00:50:39.909
I think that is something to steal from.
我认为这是可以窃取的东西。

00:50:39.909 --> 00:50:41.265
I think it was invented.
我认为它是被发明的。

00:50:41.265 --> 00:50:44.371
I can't remember if anyone in the room really did that.
我不记得房间里是否有人真的这么做过。

00:50:44.371 --> 00:50:54.367
I have a faint memory, like maybe somebody did, but we may have invented it for Marshall and Lily, who have been together long enough to have created something like that, right?
我有一个微弱的记忆，也许有人有，但我们可能是为马歇尔和莉莉发明的，他们在一起的时间足够长，创造了类似的东西，对吗？

00:50:54.367 --> 00:50:57.925
They've been, they're the old married couple in their late 20s.
他们是，他们是一对二十多岁的老夫妻。

00:50:57.925 --> 00:51:07.808
And I think they've come up with something that has helped them get this far, except we see that tactic run out of gas here in the end to it doesn't quite save them this time.
我认为他们已经想出了一些东西来帮助他们走到这一步，除了我们看到这种策略最终耗尽了能量，这一次并不能完全拯救他们。

00:51:07.808 --> 00:51:19.949
In Red Lobster, like they can't, like pauses may be effective in some ways, but when you have that tsunami of emotions behind it, it's like you can only pause so much and and something's gonna come up and take over.
在《红龙虾》中，就像他们做不到的那样，暂停在某些方面可能是有效的，但当你背后有情绪海啸时，就好像你只能暂停这么多，然后有些东西就会出现并接管。

00:51:19.949 --> 00:51:20.909
That's a great, yeah.
那真是太棒了，是的。

00:51:20.909 --> 00:51:23.909
I love that their whole story is amazing and heartbreaking.
我喜欢他们的整个故事令人惊奇和令人心碎。

00:51:23.909 --> 00:51:25.550
They're so heartbreaking in this one.
他们在这一点上真是令人心碎。

00:51:25.550 --> 00:51:29.989
Watching them unravel and still be adorable at the same time and crying, getting lobster.
看着它们解体，同时仍然很可爱，一边哭一边吃龙虾。

00:51:29.989 --> 00:51:32.550
And still loving each other so much, yeah.
而且仍然如此相爱，是的。

00:51:32.550 --> 00:51:33.949
Still loving each other.
依然彼此相爱。

00:51:33.949 --> 00:51:36.630
It's not that, it's not that they're not in love.
不是这样，不是他们不相爱。

00:51:36.630 --> 00:51:40.550
It's just they're discovering they're in two different places at this moment.
只是他们发现此时此刻他们身处两个不同的地方。

00:51:40.550 --> 00:51:42.750
And that's really fucking hard and really scary.
这真的很难也很可怕。

00:51:42.750 --> 00:51:44.469
They've been in the same place till now.
到现在为止他们一直在同一个地方。

00:51:44.469 --> 00:51:55.070
You know what was really startling to me and really beautiful was Ali, just the ferociousness with which he kisses him to get him to stop.
你知道真正让我吃惊、真正美丽的是阿里，只是他为了让他停下来而凶猛地亲吻他。

00:51:55.070 --> 00:51:57.710
I was gonna say, yeah, I really felt like.
我想说，是的，我真的这么想。

00:51:57.710 --> 00:51:59.429
And you think it's gonna work.
你认为它会起作用。

00:51:59.429 --> 00:52:00.429
You think it's gonna work?
你认为这会起作用吗？

00:52:00.429 --> 00:52:01.789
That's the great part about that.
这就是最重要的部分。

00:52:01.789 --> 00:52:02.630
Yeah.
是的。

00:52:02.630 --> 00:52:03.630
You think that's the end of the story?
你认为故事就这样结束了吗？

00:52:03.630 --> 00:52:04.469
Yeah.
是的。

00:52:04.469 --> 00:52:05.710
That's what's so good about that moment.
这就是那一刻的美妙之处。

00:52:05.710 --> 00:52:08.909
But I just love the passion.
但我就是喜欢这种激情。

00:52:08.909 --> 00:52:11.469
Like you see that this is not like an old married couple.
你看，这不像是一对老夫妻。

00:52:11.469 --> 00:52:13.710
Like they're still really hot for each other.
就像他们对彼此仍然很热情一样。

00:52:13.710 --> 00:52:17.510
they're still really, they still really feel it.
他们仍然真的，他们仍然真正感受到它。

00:52:17.510 --> 00:52:25.750
And, you know, it's also a kind of, it's a young tactic to try to like, I can't, we can't talk about this anymore.
而且，你知道，这也是一种年轻的策略，试图喜欢，我不能，我们不能再谈论这个了。

00:52:25.750 --> 00:52:27.269
Let's just go have sex, you know?
我们去做爱吧，知道吗？

00:52:27.269 --> 00:52:28.510
Like it's, I mean, sometimes that can work.
我的意思是，有时这会起作用。

00:52:28.510 --> 00:52:29.670
Let's bang our way out of this.
让我们想办法摆脱这个困境吧。

00:52:29.670 --> 00:52:32.150
Yeah, let's bang our way out of this, exactly.
是的，让我们设法摆脱这个困境。

00:52:32.150 --> 00:52:37.510
Sometimes that can work, but it is, it's a great mislead for the audience.
有时这可能有效，但事实是，这对观众来说是一个很大的误导。

00:52:37.510 --> 00:52:46.510
But I think, I think, you know, whenever you guys threw us these big, dramatic scenes that almost could be in a play on Broadway, you know?
但我想，我想，你知道，每当你们向我们展示这些大而戏剧性的场景时，这些场景几乎可能出现在百老汇的戏剧中，你知道吗？

00:52:46.510 --> 00:52:54.650
Like they get to, you can see that, that Neil, and I'm not Neil, Jason and Ali are just like, they're so present with each other.
就像他们所达到的那样，你可以看到，尼尔，我不是尼尔，杰森和阿里就像，他们彼此如此亲密。

00:52:54.650 --> 00:53:07.030
They're so, they're so, they're just acting their fucking asses off and it's such a pleasure to watch us and my comrades take this so seriously, you know?
他们就是这样，他们就是这样，他们只是在胡闹，很高兴看到我们和我的战友们如此认真地对待这件事，你知道吗？

00:53:07.030 --> 00:53:10.469
No one was phoning this stuff in, especially in scenes like that.
没有人打电话过来，尤其是在这样的场景中。

00:53:10.469 --> 00:53:13.190
I remember feeling heartbroken watching that, right?
我记得我当时看的时候心都碎了，对吗？

00:53:13.190 --> 00:53:16.349
And I remember the whole crew, it's like, it was funeral, right?
我记得整个剧组，就像，那是葬礼，对吧？

00:53:16.349 --> 00:53:20.110
It was like, we all love them so much and we're watching them fall apart.
就像，我们都非常爱他们，却眼睁睁地看着他们分崩离析。

00:53:20.110 --> 00:53:29.550
And it goes silent on the stage because you feel everybody on that crew caring about this couple so much who are not real people, but it feels so real.
舞台上一片寂静，因为你感觉到剧组中的每个人都非常关心这对夫妇，虽然他们不是真实的人，但感觉如此真实。

00:53:29.550 --> 00:53:39.070
And that's, those are the moments when you feel like the boom mic operator, when you feel the camera operator, when you feel them leaning in like, holy shit, I can't believe they're having this big of a fight.
那就是，当你感觉自己像吊杆麦克风操作员时，当你感觉摄像机操作员时，当你感觉他们靠过来时，天哪，我不敢相信他们正在打这么大的仗。

00:53:39.070 --> 00:53:45.150
Those are the moments you're like, this may be a TV show, this may go a few seasons because we're all fucking in here.
那些时刻你会想，这可能是一部电视节目，这可能会持续几季，因为我们都他妈的在这里。

00:53:45.150 --> 00:53:50.230
We had to depend on our crew as our first fans and our first viewers.
我们必须依靠我们的工作人员作为我们的第一批粉丝和第一批观众。

00:53:50.230 --> 00:54:09.909
And like, I remember like how much Bruce loved the show, how much rabbit loved the show, how much just people would tell you they'd been on enough shows to know, you know when you're on a turkey, you know when you're on a show that you're kind of like, this is a paycheck and it's gonna be good, I'm gonna feed my family and this is the business I'm in and it's fine.
就像，我记得布鲁斯有多喜欢这个节目，兔子有多喜欢这个节目，人们会告诉你他们有多么喜欢这个节目，他们已经看过足够多的节目了，你知道当你在火鸡上时，你知道当你在一个你喜欢的节目中，这是一份薪水，它会很好，我要养活我的家人，这就是我从事的生意，这很好。

00:54:09.909 --> 00:54:15.230
But it's extra special when you get to do the same job in love what you're filming, love the sound you're catching.
但当你做同样的工作并热爱你所拍摄的内容、热爱你捕捉到的声音时，那就更特别了。

00:54:15.230 --> 00:54:18.630
And the crew was always, they were so invested.
工作人员总是非常投入。

00:54:18.630 --> 00:54:27.909
And I think the thing with Marshall and Lily is, you know, Ted was just up, he was down, he was having victories, he was getting punched around.
我认为马歇尔和莉莉的问题是，你知道，特德刚刚上升，他下降了，他取得了胜利，他遭到了殴打。

00:54:27.909 --> 00:54:32.909
He was really the one who was always kind of up in the air.
他确实是那个总是高高在上的人。

00:54:33.030 --> 00:54:35.469
Barney is, you know, doing his Barney stuff.
你知道，巴尼正在做他的巴尼事情。

00:54:35.469 --> 00:54:37.349
Robin was trying to figure out who she was.
罗宾试图弄清楚她是谁。

00:54:37.349 --> 00:54:47.469
If there's one thing you could depend upon emotionally in the first season, you're like, well, if anything in this crazy world of how we met your mother is solid, it's this Marshall and Lily couple.
如果说在第一季中你可以在情感上依赖一件事，那么你会想，好吧，如果在这个疯狂的世界里，我们如何遇见你母亲的任何事情是可靠的，那就是马歇尔和莉莉夫妇。

00:54:47.469 --> 00:54:55.469
Like they give us hope that you can make it work, you can have a relationship, and the moment Ted gets his victory, that relationship falls apart.
就像他们给我们带来希望一样，你可以让它发挥作用，你可以建立一种关系，而当特德获得胜利的那一刻，这种关系就崩溃了。

00:54:55.469 --> 00:55:05.869
And it's quite devastating because it pulled the rug out from under one of the big promises of the show, is there's one couple that really is here to stay.
这是相当具有毁灭性的，因为它从节目的一项重大承诺中拉出了地毯，是否有一对夫妇真的会留下来。

00:55:05.869 --> 00:55:08.030
Yeah, and I think it's very seismic.
是的，我认为这是非常震撼的。

00:55:08.030 --> 00:55:10.670
It's seismic for the group, it's seismic for everybody.
这对整个团队来说是震撼的，对每个人来说都是震撼的。

00:55:10.670 --> 00:55:18.869
The fact that it's pouring down rain feels like somehow both Ted and the end of Marshall and the Lee's relationship both made it rain in different ways.
倾盆大雨的事实感觉就像泰德和马歇尔与李的关系的结束都以不同的方式让下雨一样。

00:55:20.389 --> 00:55:23.150
And it's huge, it's a huge moment.
这是一个伟大的时刻。

00:55:23.150 --> 00:55:29.469
It's a very powerful feeling moment and I feel that power there acting is so powerful, you're acting is so powerful.
这是一个非常强大的感觉时刻，我觉得表演的力量是如此强大，你的表演是如此强大。

00:55:29.469 --> 00:55:38.289
You guys meeting up in these completely different moments, you're almost under two different rain clouds sitting on one stoop, it's so powerful.
你们在这些完全不同的时刻相遇，你们几乎坐在一个台阶上，坐在两片不同的雨云下，真是太强大了。

00:55:38.289 --> 00:55:44.349
That moment, it's one of my favorite moments of the entire series, you and Jason, sitting there on that front stoop.
那一刻，是整个系列中我最喜欢的时刻之一，你和杰森坐在前廊上。

00:55:44.349 --> 00:55:47.849
And yeah, it's great, I loved that.
是的，这太棒了，我喜欢那个。

00:55:47.849 --> 00:55:49.110
I loved the ending of that episode.
我喜欢那集的结局。

00:55:49.110 --> 00:55:51.269
It's one of my all-time favorites.
这是我一直以来的最爱之一。

00:55:51.269 --> 00:55:55.460
Well, this is a section, a segment of the show we like to call.
嗯，这是我们喜欢称之为的节目的一个部分。

00:55:55.460 --> 00:56:03.739
Questions and observations from a clinical psychologist who's never seen how I met your mother and also happens to be married to Josh.
来自一位临床心理学家的问题和观察，他从未见过我是如何认识你母亲的，而且恰好与乔什结婚了。

00:56:03.739 --> 00:56:17.539
I saw a friend this weekend who loves him and she listened to some of my questions and she said, okay, that's fine, but what I really want to hear about is what it's like for you to watch Josh on TV.
这个周末我见到了一位爱他的朋友，她听了我的一些问题，她说，好吧，没关系，但我真正想听的是你在电视上看乔什的感觉。

00:56:17.539 --> 00:56:19.420
So I'll answer that.
所以我会回答这个问题。

00:56:19.420 --> 00:56:24.420
I'll say it's thrilling and also somewhat surreal.
我想说这很令人兴奋，也有点超现实。

00:56:24.420 --> 00:56:33.699
First of all, and I tell Josh this all the time, I think he's an extraordinary actor and hilarious and I'm so proud when watching him in his craft.
首先，我一直告诉乔什，我认为他是一位出色的演员，而且很搞笑，当我看到他的表演时，我感到非常自豪。

00:56:33.699 --> 00:56:44.179
And it's a very special thing to laugh at something he's done on TV or face he's made and simultaneously get to hold his hand.
嘲笑他在电视上所做的事情或他所做的事情，同时握住他的手，这是一件非常特别的事情。

00:56:44.179 --> 00:56:46.300
But it's also very strange, you know?
但这也很奇怪，你知道吗？

00:56:46.300 --> 00:57:15.420
I feel like I'm watching my husband, but not my husband, watching a version of him without the 20 years of wisdom that he's gained over time and has now, you know, I'm watching not yet the man that I met but the seeds of that man and I'm also watching him play a version of a character that he has some overlap with but is he's ultimately very different from Ted.
我觉得我在看我的丈夫，但不是我的丈夫，看他的一个版本，没有他随着时间的推移获得的20年的智慧，你知道，我看的还不是我遇到的那个人，而是那个人的种子，我也在看他扮演一个与他有一些重叠的角色的版本，但他最终与特德有很大不同。

00:57:16.059 --> 00:57:30.380
So it's him but it's not him which is something we've unpacked and talked about a lot what it was like to be known as someone called the name of someone that he wasn't.
所以是他，但又不是他，这是我们已经解开的东西，并谈论了很多被称为某人的名字的感觉，而他不是。

00:57:30.380 --> 00:57:42.260
So as his wife, I'm personally glad I didn't see the show before we met and I'm glad I waited this long to watch so that I never get confused, I never conflate the two.
因此，作为他的妻子，我个人很高兴在我们见面之前没有看到这个节目，我很高兴我等了这么久才观看，这样我就不会感到困惑，我永远不会将两者混为一谈。

00:57:42.260 --> 00:57:48.900
I know who Josh is and I know who Ted is and now I feel like I just get to love them both.
我知道乔希是谁，我知道泰德是谁，现在我觉得我开始爱他们俩了。

00:57:48.900 --> 00:57:49.900
Yeah.
是的。

00:57:49.900 --> 00:57:50.900
That's the best.
那是最好的。

00:57:50.900 --> 00:57:51.900
How does that feel Josh?
乔什感觉如何？

00:57:51.900 --> 00:57:53.460
That's the best, that's the podcast, right?
这是最好的，这就是播客，对吧？

00:57:53.460 --> 00:57:55.420
That's the, this came from that, right?
就是这个，这个是从那个来的，对吧？

00:57:55.420 --> 00:57:59.059
This podcast that we're doing came from that in some way.
我们正在做的这个播客在某种程度上就是源于此。

00:57:59.059 --> 00:58:00.059
Did it not?
不是吗？

00:58:00.059 --> 00:58:02.260
Isn't that, that's the spark in some ways?
这难道不是某种意义上的火花吗？

00:58:02.260 --> 00:58:03.260
Yeah.
是的。

00:58:03.260 --> 00:58:06.449
Yeah, she said, I want to watch it.
是的，她说，我想看。

00:58:06.449 --> 00:58:08.730
I'm ready to watch it.
我已经准备好观看了。

00:58:08.730 --> 00:58:17.889
And so then I thought, you know, I called you, I said, you want to do something more formal because I'm going to start watching the show with Jordana.
然后我想，你知道，我打电话给你，我说，你想做一些更正式的事情，因为我要开始和乔丹娜一起看节目。

00:58:17.889 --> 00:59:10.900
I think I had listened to that voice note, but I was very moved just now and, you know, I really, I think I'm getting to see my, you know, I think a real love is you get to see yourself through someone else's eyes, through someone else's very loving eyes, you get to feel yourself worthy of that kind of love and I think that getting to watch the show with her and her being so clear on the boundaries and borders of my identity and who I am and who she met and who she fell in love with and who she's with now but also that he had this big life before her and this big very public facing aspect to that life.
我想我听到了那个声音，但我刚才很感动，你知道，我真的，我想我开始看到我的，你知道，我认为真正的爱是你通过别人的眼睛看到自己，通过别人充满爱的眼睛，你会觉得自己值得那种爱，我认为和她一起看节目，她对我的身份和我是谁，她遇到了谁以及她爱上了谁非常清楚。以及她现在和谁在一起，而且他在她面前有一个伟大的生活，以及这个生活中非常公开的一面。

00:59:10.900 --> 00:59:17.780
And just the ability to, yeah, to sit with her and show her, this is what I was doing.
是的，能够坐在她身边并向她展示，这就是我正在做的事情。

00:59:17.780 --> 00:59:27.099
This is what helped me become the kind of man who could marry you and be here with you on this couch in Brooklyn watching this show.
这帮助我成为了那种可以嫁给你并和你一起坐在布鲁克林的沙发上看这个节目的男人。

00:59:27.099 --> 00:59:39.059
It's very sweet and she, as you can hear, I guess I also really want to thank Jordana one for wanting to watch the show again, wanting to learn about this part of me.
这非常甜蜜，正如你所听到的，我想我也真的很想感谢乔丹娜想再次观看这个节目，想了解我的这一部分。

00:59:39.059 --> 00:59:47.739
And also, for her contributions week to week, I think they've really elevated the show.
而且，对于她每周的贡献，我认为他们确实提升了这部剧的水平。

00:59:47.739 --> 00:59:54.940
I think they've invited us, me, you and Craig and Alec, to go deeper in thinking about the show.
我认为他们邀请了我们、我、你、克雷格和亚历克，更深入地思考这个节目。

00:59:54.940 --> 01:00:04.380
I think she comes at this from such a pure, but really informed and thoughtful place.
我认为她是从一个如此纯粹、但又真正见多识广、深思熟虑的地方得出这一结论的。

01:00:04.380 --> 01:00:09.619
I love that my wife is both psychological and spiritual.
我爱我的妻子，无论是心理上还是精神上。

01:00:09.619 --> 01:00:15.539
She has these two qualities that were very important for me in partnership because I have those in myself.
她拥有这两种品质，这对我在合作中非常重要，因为我自己也具备这些品质。

01:00:15.539 --> 01:00:26.380
So it's been a real treat to both watch the show next to her and also hear her reflections on them.
因此，能够在她旁边观看节目并听到她对他们的看法真是一种享受。

01:00:26.380 --> 01:00:31.460
She goes into our office and I think thinks for a little while and records these for us.
她走进我们的办公室，我想她想了一会儿并为我们记录了这些。

01:00:31.460 --> 01:00:36.260
But it's been a real asset to the show to have her.
但拥有她对这部剧来说是一笔真正的财富。

01:00:36.260 --> 01:00:38.880
She doesn't, she would die if we ever had her on as a guest.
她不，如果我们邀请她做客人，她就会死。

01:00:38.880 --> 01:00:44.300
She would freeze up and she doesn't like to be put on the spot, but she really likes to contribute in this particular way.
她会僵住，她不喜欢被置于困境，但她真的很喜欢以这种特殊的方式做出贡献。

01:00:44.300 --> 01:00:46.380
So it's been, it's been just lovely.
就这样，这真是太可爱了。

01:00:46.380 --> 01:00:53.179
So thanks, thank you, Jordana, for, for upping the quality of this show so considerably.
所以谢谢乔丹娜，谢谢你大大提高了这个节目的质量。

01:00:53.179 --> 01:00:55.019
And I love you.
我爱你。

01:00:55.019 --> 01:01:04.139
As, as we're heading towards the end of this, Josh, I want to ask you a question about this whole venture that you have set us on here, this, these, these podcasts.
乔什，当我们即将结束时，我想问你一个关于你让我们在这里建立的整个项目的问题，这个，这些，这些播客。

01:01:04.139 --> 01:01:11.596
I went back and I listened to the trailer, which we took much of from our very first and second episodes.
我回去听了预告片，我们从第一集和第二集中摘取了很多内容。

01:01:11.596 --> 01:01:18.342
And the last line of the trailer is you saying, it's like, are you ready to go back to Titanic?
预告片的最后一句是你说，你准备好回到泰坦尼克号了吗？

01:01:18.342 --> 01:01:23.241
And I am as a huge Titanic fan who, yes, saw it twice on opening day.
我是泰坦尼克号的铁杆粉丝，是的，我在首映当天看了两遍。

01:01:23.241 --> 01:01:28.780
And yesterday, went to see the virtual reality experience here in Los Angeles.
昨天，去洛杉矶这里参观了虚拟现实体验。

01:01:28.780 --> 01:01:33.538
I'm reminded, of course, that at the end, I know, holds for a plus.
当然，有人提醒我，我知道，最后还是有好处的。

01:01:33.538 --> 01:01:43.763
At the end of Titanic, I'm reminded that he didn't find what he was looking for, or more importantly, what he says is, I never let it in, right?
在《泰坦尼克号》的结尾，我想起他没有找到他要找的东西，或者更重要的是，他说的是，我从来没有让它进来，对吧？

01:01:43.763 --> 01:01:48.379
He discovered something new when he finally went back to Titanic.
当他最终回到泰坦尼克号时，他发现了一些新东西。

01:01:48.379 --> 01:02:03.859
So my question to you, Josh, is, is there something new that you never expected rewatching all of this and talking about it that it would do for you, or even more titanically, is there something that you're letting in?
所以我问你的问题是，乔什，是否有一些新的东西是你从未预料到的，重新观看这一切并谈论它会对你有所帮助，或者更重要的是，是否有一些你正在允许的东西？

01:02:03.860 --> 01:02:12.360
Is there something that you've let in that maybe you haven't over the last decade or 20 years that doing this has done for you?
有没有什么事情是你在过去十年或二十年里没有做到的，而这样做却给你带来了好处？

01:02:12.360 --> 01:02:13.840
Yeah, what a great question, Alec.
是的，这是一个很好的问题，亚历克。

01:02:13.840 --> 01:02:19.159
I think that I've mentioned this before, but it bears repeating.
我想我之前已经提到过这一点，但值得重复一遍。

01:02:19.159 --> 01:02:32.164
I have always had a conflicted relationship with this show insofar as it gave me so much it also took away something that I didn't know I had and was precious.
我和这个节目一直有一种矛盾的关系，因为它给了我很多，但也带走了一些我不知道自己拥有的、珍贵的东西。

01:02:32.164 --> 01:02:45.659
My anonymity, my ability to be thought of as a singular, unified personality, Josh Radner, it bifurcated that and split that up in certain ways that were unsettling.
我的匿名性，我有能力被认为是一个独特的、统一的人格，乔什·拉德纳，它把它分成了两部分，并以某些令人不安的方式分裂了它。

01:02:45.659 --> 01:02:51.466
It gave me an identity crisis over the years that was hard to navigate.
多年来，它给我带来了难以应对的身份危机。

01:02:51.466 --> 01:03:07.578
And it gave me, it was my patron, it allowed me to live a creative life and do all these different things and explore these different parts of myself and travel and expand my world in so many ways.
它给了我，它是我的赞助人，它让我过上创造性的生活，做所有这些不同的事情，探索自己的不同部分，旅行并以多种方式扩展我的世界。

01:03:07.578 --> 01:03:23.200
It also, one of my favorite things is if I reach out to someone that I love their work or if I just admire them, whether it's show business or not, but my reputation precedes me a little bit.
而且，我最喜欢的事情之一是，如果我接触到我喜欢他们的工作的人，或者我只是钦佩他们，无论是否是演艺界，但我的声誉比我先一点。

01:03:23.199 --> 01:03:27.480
And people generally say, thanks for reaching out, let's have coffee.
人们通常会说，谢谢你伸出援手，我们喝咖啡吧。

01:03:27.480 --> 01:03:37.079
So it allows me to be in the world in a pretty dynamic way that I've always really loved, but there's always been this shadow side to it.
所以它让我能够以一种我一直非常喜欢的充满活力的方式生活在这个世界上，但它总是有阴影的一面。

01:03:37.079 --> 01:03:40.719
And it's always been this thing that I kind of was swatting away.
我一直在努力摆脱这种东西。

01:03:40.719 --> 01:03:49.719
If someone came up to me and called me by my real name, I was so much friendlier than if they called me Ted.
如果有人走到我面前并用我的真名称呼我，我会比他们叫我特德友好得多。

01:03:50.400 --> 01:03:55.039
or if they shouted Ted from their car, I would bristle and I would be angry about it.
或者如果他们在车里对特德大喊大叫，我会感到愤怒，我会对此感到愤怒。

01:03:55.039 --> 01:04:02.719
But I've done a lot of work around it and I was doing a lot of really good work around that before we even did this show.
但我已经围绕这个问题做了很多工作，而且在我们做这个节目之前我已经做了很多非常好的工作。

01:04:02.719 --> 01:04:13.960
Getting married helped me a lot with it just because my wife is a psychologist and she was so acutely and beautifully sensitive to this particular kind of sensitivity that I had around this.
结婚对我帮助很大，因为我的妻子是一名心理学家，她对我在这方面的这种特殊敏感性非常敏锐和美丽。

01:04:13.960 --> 01:04:32.980
So I was moving through it and past it and integrating it more and more, but the process of revisiting the show and watching the show, I think I was able to, you know, we talked about this on a commercial, but I was just in Ecuador last weekend or the weekend before for a Comic Con.
所以我不断地经历它、超越它，并越来越多地整合它，但在重新审视节目和观看节目的过程中，我想我能够，你知道，我们在广告中讨论过这一点，但我上周末或前周末刚刚在厄瓜多尔参加动漫展。

01:04:32.980 --> 01:04:45.789
I was there for three or four days and I met a ton of people all from Ecuador and they were so lovely and so such beautiful people and they were so excited to see me and meet me.
我在那里呆了三四天，遇到了很多来自厄瓜多尔的人，他们非常可爱，非常美丽，他们很高兴见到我并见到我。

01:04:45.789 --> 01:04:51.010
And it wasn't all just about the show, they seemed to really see me in a kind of 360 way.
这不仅仅与演出有关，他们似乎真的以一种 360 度的方式来看待我。

01:04:51.010 --> 01:04:56.530
But meeting people who just said, you know, I love you so much.
但遇到的人只是说，你知道，我非常爱你。

01:04:56.530 --> 01:04:59.050
You taught me so much about life.
你教会了我很多关于生活的知识。

01:04:59.050 --> 01:05:01.010
You inspire me.
你激励我。

01:05:01.010 --> 01:05:03.949
I don't know what my life would be without Ted Mosby in this show.
我不知道如果没有泰德·莫斯比在这部剧中我的生活会怎样。

01:05:03.949 --> 01:05:06.010
Like I could hear it with different ears.
就像我可以用不同的耳朵听到一样。

01:05:06.010 --> 01:05:12.570
I could hear it with, I could hear it that they weren't saying you're just this one thing.
我可以听到，我可以听到他们并没有说你只是一件事。

01:05:12.570 --> 01:05:14.909
They were saying, you changed my life.
他们说，你改变了我的生活。

01:05:14.909 --> 01:05:18.269
you were a part of and the energy you brought to it changed my life.
你是其中的一部分，你为它带来的能量改变了我的生活。

01:05:18.269 --> 01:05:26.710
And that to me has been, I'm so glad that my ears are open enough and my heart is now open enough to hear that.
对我来说，我很高兴我的耳朵足够开放，我的心现在足够开放来听到这些。

01:05:26.710 --> 01:05:41.510
And you know, we talked about this, Craig, I remember we were saying like, sometimes people when they're such big fans of the show and they come up to you about the show, it almost feels like hostile or aggressive like, like they're being a little like me or it's tinged with some kind of edge.
你知道，我们讨论过这个，克雷格，我记得我们说过，有时候，当人们是这个节目的忠实粉丝时，他们向你询问这个节目，这几乎让人感觉充满敌意或侵略性，就像他们有点像我，或者带有某种边缘。

01:05:41.510 --> 01:05:46.130
And I felt that for years, but now I'm just seeing the sweetness of it.
多年来我一直有这种感觉，但现在我才看到它的甜蜜。

01:05:46.130 --> 01:05:56.409
And I'm seeing, you know, when a 14 or 15 year old comes up to me and I'm like, you weren't born when this show started, but you love it and it's teaching you something.
我发现，你知道，当一个 14 或 15 岁的孩子走到我面前时，我会想，这个节目开始时你还没有出生，但你喜欢它，它教会了你一些东西。

01:05:56.409 --> 01:06:08.489
And I think that even though we're doing some Mia Culpas around some stuff we did, I think 85% of this show is like, we stand by it and we believe that we were imparting something beautiful.
我认为，尽管我们围绕我们所做的一些事情做了一些弥亚罪责，但我认为这个节目的 85% 都是这样的，我们坚持它，我们相信我们正在传递一些美丽的东西。

01:06:08.489 --> 01:06:10.530
We are putting something beautiful into the culture.
我们正在将一些美丽的东西融入到文化中。

01:06:10.530 --> 01:06:12.369
And it remains.
它仍然存在。

01:06:12.369 --> 01:06:15.090
And I feel really grateful to have been a part of something like that.
我真的很感激能够参与这样的事情。

01:06:15.090 --> 01:06:18.010
Some of those tender spots still remain.
其中一些痛点仍然存在。

01:06:18.010 --> 01:06:24.130
If you see me on the street, you'll have a much nicer encounter with me if you call me Josh.
如果你在街上看到我，如果你叫我乔什，你会和我有更好的接触。

01:06:24.130 --> 01:06:25.130
You just will.
你就会的。

01:06:25.130 --> 01:06:30.969
I still have some sensitivities towards like, call me by my real name.
我对“请用真名呼唤我”之类的东西还是有一些敏感的。

01:06:30.969 --> 01:06:39.230
But largely, I'm just able to understand why people love this show so much.
但很大程度上，我只能理解为什么人们如此喜欢这个节目。

01:06:39.230 --> 01:06:54.949
And I'm getting to be, I think I've mentioned this, but Paul McCartney said, you know, when people come up to him and say, I'm such a fan of The Beatles and he says, oh, me too, you know, he was both a part of The Beatles and a huge driving force of why it was so brilliant and successful.
我想我已经提到过这一点，但是保罗·麦卡特尼说，你知道，当人们走到他面前说，我是披头士乐队的粉丝时，他说，哦，我也是，你知道，他既是披头士乐队的一部分，也是披头士乐队如此辉煌和成功的巨大推动力。

01:06:54.949 --> 01:06:59.030
And also he can hover outside it now and go, wow, this was a phenomenon.
他现在也可以在它外面徘徊，然后说，哇，这真是一个现象。

01:06:59.030 --> 01:07:05.469
We were carried aloft by something much bigger than just me and my own talent, my own song writing ability.
我们被一些比我自己和我自己的才华、我自己的歌曲创作能力更重要的东西所支撑。

01:07:05.469 --> 01:07:06.469
This was bigger than me.
这比我还大。

01:07:06.469 --> 01:07:14.070
I'm a fan of the thing that I was a part of, which is a very strange thing to say, I'm a fan of this thing I was a part of, you know?
我是我参与过的事情的粉丝，这说起来很奇怪，我是我参与过的事情的粉丝，你知道吗？

01:07:14.070 --> 01:07:18.590
If I suspect if I wasn't on how I met your mother, I would have really, really loved it.
如果我怀疑如果不是我是如何认识你母亲的，我会真的非常喜欢它。

01:07:18.590 --> 01:07:21.460
I was actually thinking about this, Craig.
我实际上在想这个，克雷格。

01:07:21.460 --> 01:07:33.460
I was thinking, what are the chances of meeting two guys who were mostly my age, who both went to, we all went to small liberal arts colleges.
我在想，遇到两个和我年龄差不多的人，我们都上过小型文理学院的机会有多大。

01:07:33.460 --> 01:07:36.340
One of them was from Ohio, where I was from.
其中一位来自俄亥俄州，我也来自那里。

01:07:36.340 --> 01:07:42.619
We have the same taste in music, the same taste in movies, the same kind of concerns in the world.
我们对音乐有同样的品味，对电影有同样的品味，对世界有同样的关注。

01:07:42.619 --> 01:07:53.659
We both wanted, we all want our lives to be magic and creative and funny and deep and philosophical.
我们都希望，我们都希望我们的生活充满魔力、富有创造力、有趣、深刻、富有哲理。

01:07:53.860 --> 01:07:56.900
We want to interrogate what we're doing here.
